      "title": "Optimal Control of Fluid Dosing in Hemorrhage Resuscitation",
      "authors": [
        "Jacob Grant",
        "Hossein Mirinejad"
      ],
      "abstract": "Fluid resuscitation, also called fluid therapy, is commonly used in treatment\nof critical care patients. Of the variables inherent to fluid therapy\ntreatment, fluid infusion types are well-studied, but volumetric optimization\nis not. Automated fluid resuscitation systems employ computational control\nalgorithms along with physiological sensors and actuators to automatically\nadjust the fluid infusion dosage in hemorrhage scenarios. Most automated\nhemorrhage resuscitation control algorithms implemented to date are of empiric\nnature and not effective in the presence of clinical disturbances. This work\npresents preliminary results of a novel controller based on optimal control\napproach for automated fluid therapy systems. A receding horizon controller is\ndesigned to automatically adjust fluid infusion rate in hemorrhagic situations.\nThe proposed control approach aims to address the inefficiency of current\nresuscitation control algorithms in finding the accurate fluid dosage\nadjustments. Performance of the proposed approach is compared against a\nproportional-integral-derivative (PID) controller for a simulated hemorrhage\nscenario.",
      "categories": [
        "eess.SY",
        "cs.SY"
      ],
      "published": "2023-12-11T16:53:02+00:00",
      "url": "http://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.06521v1",
      "resource_uri": "arxiv://2312.06521v1"
    }
  ]
}

